ons/BernoulliB/07/"}],"label":"41","misc":["The Wolfram Functions Site"]}}],"number_of_pages":30,"legacy_creation_date":"2016-12-23","preprint_date":"2016-12-22","author_count":2,"public_notes":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"17+18 pages, 1 figure. v2: references added, an implicit assumption dropped, 2nd law proof remains non-perturbative, local 2nd law proof now perturbative in derivative expansion"}],"earliest_date":"2016-12-22","facet_author_name":["1000027_Hong Liu","1507746_Paolo Glorioso"],"core":true,"license":[{"imposing":"arXiv","url":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/"}],"_oai":{"sets":["Literature"],"id":"oai:inspirehep.net:1505646","updated":"2024-08-28T00:44:20.615664"},"arxiv_eprints":[{"categories":["hep-th","cond-mat.stat-mech","math-ph","math.MP"],"value":"1612.07705"}],"referenced_authors_bais":["Lam.Hui.1","Sachin.Jain.1","Julian.Seymour.Schwinger.1","M.Campisi.1","J.Bhattacharya.1","Thomas.Gregoire.1","Riccardo.Rattazzi.1","Kuang.chao.Chou.1","M.Rangamani.1","M.Harder.2","L.M.Sieberer.1","Sayantani.Bhattacharyya.1","J.Polonyi.1","A.Gambassi.1","L.P.Kadanoff.1","H.G.Moser.1","N.Banerjee.1","L.Yu.10","P.Hanggi.1","G.E.Crooks.2","H.K.Janssen.2","S.Grozdanov.1","Richard.P.Feynman.1","A.Nicolis.1","A.Chiocchetta.1","R.Folk.2","P.C.Hohenberg.1","L.V.Keldysh.1","F.L.Vernon.1","P.Talkner.1","G.D.Moore.1","P.Kovtun.1","P.Romatschke.1","S.L.Dubovsky.1","P.Glorioso.1","T.Sharma.2","A.Ritz.1","S.Minwalla.1","R.Loganayagam.1","M.P.Heller.1","R.Meyer.2","A.Yarom.1","B.l.Hao.3","M.Crossley.1","Bertrand.I.Halperin.1","C.Jarzynski.1","Natalia.Pinzani.Fokeeva.1","U.C.Taeuber.1","J.de.Boer.1","F.M.Haehl.1","S.Diehl.1","P.Martin.3","Z.B.Su.4","M.Kaminski.1","Hong.Liu.1","Kristan.Jensen.1","D.T.Son.1"],"figures":[{"filename":"nfig1.png","caption":"Closed time path contour in a state given by density matrix $\\rho_0$. The two lines represent path integrals along two copies of spacetime with arrows indicating the direction of integrations. (a) and (b) correspond to having $\\rho_0$ as the initial and final state respectively.","source":"arxiv","key":"b3f326da6b4d247152ee02125a805e9d","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/b3f326da6b4d247152ee02125a805e9d"}],"legacy_version":"20170220045253.0","inspire_categories":[{"term":"Theory-HEP"},{"term":"General Physics"},{"term":"Math and Math Physics"},{"term":"Condensed Matter","source":"arxiv"}],"first_author":{"full_name":"Glorioso, Paolo","last_name":"Glorioso","first_name":"Paolo","recid":1507746},"control_number":1505646,"document_type":["article"],"texkeys":["Glorioso:2016gsa"],"abstracts":[{"source":"arXiv","value":"The second law of thermodynamics states that for a thermally isolated system entropy never decreases. Most physical processes we observe in nature involve variations of macroscopic quantities over spatial and temporal scales much larger than microscopic molecular collision scales and thus can be considered as in local equilibrium. For a many-body system in local equilibrium a stronger version of the second law applies which says that the entropy production at each spacetime point should be non-negative. In this paper we provide a proof of the second law for such systems and a first derivation of the local second law. For this purpose we develop a general non-equilibrium effective field theory of slow degrees of freedom from integrating out fast degrees of freedom in a quantum many-body system and consider its classical limit. The key elements of the proof are the presence of a $Z_2$ symmetry, which can be considered as a proxy for local equilibrium and micro-time-reversibility, and a classical remnant of quantum unitarity. The $Z_2$ symmetry leads to a local current from a procedure analogous to that used in the Noether theorem. Unitarity leads to a definite sign of the divergence of the current. We also discuss the origin of an arrow of time, as well as the coincidence of causal and thermodynamical arrows of time. Applied to hydrodynamics, the proof gives a first-principle derivation of the phenomenological entropy current condition and provides a constructive procedure for obtaining the entropy current.","abstract_source_suggest":{"input":"arXiv"}}],"primary_arxiv_category":["hep-th"],"titles":[{"source":"arXiv","title":"The second law of thermodynamics from symmetry and unitarity"}],"curated":true},"links":{"bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1505646?format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1505646?format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1505646?format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1505646?format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1505646?format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/1505646?format=cv","citations":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=refersto%3Arecid%3A1505646"},"created":"2016-12-23T00:00:00+00:00","updated":"2024-08-28T00:44:20.615664+00:00","id":"1505646"}],"total":1},"links":{"self":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Glorioso%3A2016gsa&size=10&page=1","bibtex":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Glorioso%3A2016gsa&size=10&page=1&format=bibtex","latex-eu":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Glorioso%3A2016gsa&size=10&page=1&format=latex-eu","latex-us":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Glorioso%3A2016gsa&size=10&page=1&format=latex-us","json":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Glorioso%3A2016gsa&size=10&page=1&format=json","json-expanded":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Glorioso%3A2016gsa&size=10&page=1&format=json-expanded","cv":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/?q=texkey%20Glorioso%3A2016gsa&size=10&page=1&format=cv"}}